From: Akisaira <akisaira@outlook.com>
Subject: [PATCH] media: qcom: camss: Vote the rail level the clock rates need

CAMSS sets its clock rates but never votes the rail level they need, so
MMCX stays at the LOW_SVS camcc votes for itself. On SC8280XP the IFE at
558 MHz needs SVS and camnoc_axi, which the VFE sets alongside, needs
TURBO at 480 MHz. With the display on, the display controller holds MMCX
at TURBO_L1 and hides this; with the display off, the full size pixel
path overflows from the first frame and has also reset the SoC.

Let platforms list the rail level per clock rate. camss_enable_clocks()
votes the highest level the subdevice's clocks need at their set rates
before enabling them, camss_disable_clocks() drops the vote after
disabling them; the votes of all subdevices are aggregated and set as
the performance state of the CAMSS power domain, which the GDSCs pass
on to MMCX. camss_disable_clocks() takes the device for this, like
camss_enable_clocks().

SC8280XP's levels are the same in the Windows PEP clock plan
(qcpep8280.sys) and in the vendor's camcc-direwolf.c, whose camcc has
the same RCG offsets and frequency tables as camcc-sc8280xp.c.

Tested with the display off: full size single and dual IFE, binned,
still and front streams; MMCX at 384 while the full size path streams,
back to 0 after; the RDI path at 400/150 MHz stays at LOW_SVS.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Origin: local
Upstream-Status: Pending
